<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alids</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alids are the descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, cousin and son-in-law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>, especially through his sons Hasan and Husayn. Many Muslims, above all the Shia, regarded them as the rightful leaders of the community, and Alid claimants led revolts against the Umayyad and Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>. Alid dynasties ruled states such as the Idrisids in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, the Zaydis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and the Ukhaydirids in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alids.jpg" alt="Alids" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Luiz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arabic_caligraphic_seal_in_Hagia_Sophia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
